{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HVtbGtCfdambEtALXUBwTn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-584\"\n3\ttitle = \"The snapshot's scoped writer and unscoped refusal disagree on registry scope\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tspecref = \"\"\n6\tbuildtier = \"medium\"\n7\tpriority = 2\n8\tsafety_class = \"spine\"\n9\tbar = \"DevStg-Impl\"\n10\t+++\n11\t\n12\t## Deliverable\n13\t\n14\tRULED (a) and built it. `baseline_snapshot.refresh_refusal` now judges the\n15\tregistries THIS ACT WOULD WRITE — `set(SNAPSHOTTED)` under `seed`, otherwise\n16\t`_authorised_registries`, the same function `copy_live` writes from — instead of\n17\tthe whole ledger, with ONE arm left unscoped: an act whose write set is empty is\n18\tjudged globally, so a refresh that would copy nothing in a tree carrying drifted\n19\tapproved text still refuses rather than exiting 0 in silence. `_refresh_targets`\n20\tpasses `seed` through; the message now names what the act DOES authorise, in\n21\tplace of the header's false \"nothing in this working tree authorises it\"; the\n22\trendering moved to a sibling `_refusal_text` because the second decision took\n23\t`refresh_refusal` to cognitive 18 and `check_complexity` went RED on it (cleared\n24\tby the extraction, no baseline bump taken).\n25\t\n26\tTHE ACCEPTANCE, driven: the disposition's own command —\n27\t`refresh_refusal('.', {low-level-requirements.toml: <ref>})` — returned `''` at\n28\tthis tip, where before it returned a refusal listing seventeen SR rows and four\n29\tTC rows the caller had not judged. Six tests in\n30\t`tests/test_baseline_snapshot.py`, each confirmed RED against the pre-change\n31\tmodule and green after; one existing test was reworked because it pinned the\n32\tdefect (it asserted a refusal naming a registry the act cannot write) and its\n33\treal intent — the pre-WI-571 short-circuit — is now asserted over the COPY,\n34\twhich is what could actually launder.\n35\t\n36\tThe scoped gate is NOT vacuous: a registry with a brand-new row arriving already\n37\t`Approved` is written without any `Status` MOVE, so an unrelated amendment could\n38\tride that anchor in. That case is refused, and is its own test.\n39\t\n40\tFull suite: `1 failed, 3366 passed, 24 skipped in 630.54s`. The one failure is\n41\tinherited — `docs/handoff-2026-09-03.md` is orphaned at the integration base too,\n42\tre-driven against a detached worktree of `794de60d`. Smoke tier `1520 passed, 8\n43\tskipped`, 36.3 s against the 60 s budget. No spine cell was minted or amended\n44\there, so no approval brief is owed. Log: `docs/log.d/WI-584-scoped-refusal.md`.\n45\t\n46\t## Context\n47\t\n48\tTHE RULING: (a). The gate is scoped to the writer. Grounds, in the row's own\n49\tterms: `copy_live`'s write set IS the authorised set (`_authorised_registries`\n50\t= the registries `--approves` names plus the ones an approving `Status` move\n51\thappened in), so a registry the refusal blocks on but the writer would not\n52\ttouch cannot be absorbed by the act being refused — the block protects nothing\n53\tand costs the act. Reading (b) would have to unwind WI-571's scoping, which\n54\tclosed a measured laundering path of its own (a spine flip re-sealing off-spine\n55\tdrift), and would contradict the ruled per-registry contract the refusal text\n56\talready states (\"authorises the one registry it names\"). The false block is\n57\talso not confined to `--approves`: a bare no-flag act carrying an approving\n58\t`Status` move in one registry is refused today by drift in another it will\n59\tnever write, which is the same defect on the commoner path.\n60\t\n61\tONE ARM SURVIVES the scoping, and is kept deliberately: when the act's write\n62\tset is EMPTY and approved text has drifted, the refusal still fires. An act\n63\tthat copies nothing is a no-op, and a no-op that exits 0 while an Approved\n64\trow's text stands rewritten is the laundering scenario answered with silence.\n65\tThat arm is what keeps `test_a_APPROVED_amendment_with_no_flip_and_no_ref_is_\n66\tREFUSED` and the de-approval regression honest under the scoped rule.\n67\t\n68\tDrafted by WI-578 (its ## Dispositions section) and minted at its merge - drafts-not-mints, ruling R1/R3.\n69\t\n70\tVERDICT THIS CONTINUES:\n71\t`docs/reviews/wi-578-adjudicate-llr-158-llr-203/001-ADJUDICATE-921f947.md`,\n72\tgoverning line `VERDICT: MEANING rows=3` over `LLR-158`, `LLR-203` and\n73\t`LLR-204`. All three are MEANING, so the flip-back arm does not apply. This\n74\tsuccessor exists because the re-attestation the rung released to the loop could\n75\tnot be TAKEN — not because the text is unblessable. WI-573 withheld the same\n76\tanchor for a content reason (`LLR-158`'s bound was false); that reason is\n77\tclosed, and what stands in the way now is the mechanism itself.\n78\t\n79\tTHE OBSERVABLE, reproducible at this row's tip. Running the prescribed act,\n80\tnaming only the registry ruled on —\n81\t\n82\t    python3 project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py snapshot \\\n83\t      --approves \"low-level-requirements.toml=<ref>\"\n84\t\n85\t— is REFUSED, and `low-level-requirements.toml` is absent from the refusal:\n86\tevery row listed is an `SR-###` or `TC-###` the caller did not judge.\n87\t`baseline_snapshot.refresh_ledger` at this commit reads\n88\t`system-requirements.toml` 17 absorbed rows, `low-level-requirements.toml` 7,\n89\t`test-cases.toml` 3, no `flips` anywhere.\n90\t\n91\tTHE CONTRADICTION. `copy_live` has been SCOPED since WI-571: it writes only the\n92\tregistries `--approves` names plus those an approving `Status` move happened in\n93\t(`_authorised_registries`), and its own docstring says \"an untouched registry is\n94\tnot written\". `refresh_refusal` builds `blocked` from the WHOLE ledger\n95\t(`if e[\"absorbed\"] and not e[\"flips\"] and rel not in named`), so it judges\n96\tregistries the refresh would not touch. WI-571 scoped the writer and left the\n97\tgate global.\n98\t\n99\tWHY IT DOES NOT SELF-CLEAR. The 17 SR rows are WI-547's CLARITY verdict, and a\n100\tCLARITY verdict owes nothing further by rule — nothing will ever authorise those\n101\tcells, so their drift is permanent by design. The 3 TC rows are WI-566's MEANING\n102\tverdict, owed on a different registry and a different lane. Under today's gate\n103\tthe LLR anchor is unreachable until someone signs for registries they did not\n104\trule on, which is the false claim this rung exists to prevent.\n105\t\n106\tIN SCOPE — RULE FIRST, THEN BUILD. Two readings are open and this row must pick\n107\tone on the evidence, not assume:\n108\t\n109\t  (a) The gate should be scoped to the writer — `blocked` restricted to the\n110\t      registries the refresh will actually write — so a per-registry\n111\t      adjudication can complete its own act and untouched registries keep both\n112\t      their stale bytes and their visible drift.\n113\t  (b) The refusal text is the intended contract (\"naming EACH registry above\"),\n114\t      the snapshot is meant to move as a whole, and what needs rethinking is\n115\t      the per-registry adjudication rung that hands one adjudicator authority\n116\t      over one registry.\n117\t\n118\tWhichever is ruled, the acceptance is the same shape: a test that a scoped,\n119\tauthorised, single-registry approval either COMPLETES (a) or is refused with a\n120\tmessage that says why a single-registry act cannot exist (b) — the current\n121\tmessage claims the caller has authorised nothing, which is false, and that\n122\talone is worth correcting under either reading.\n123\t\n124\tTHE ANCHOR IS NOT THIS ROW'S STEP (REVIEW-A round 002, finding 1). A `spine`\n125\trow is a WORKER LANE, and `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal` refuses a\n126\tlane that writes `SNAPSHOT_DIR` — by construction, at merge. So this row's\n127\twhole scope is the ruling on (a)/(b), the `refresh_refusal` change it implies,\n128\tand the test. It stays `spine` for one reason (round 003): it changes\n129\t`refresh_refusal`, the gate every approval act consults, so it runs alone and\n130\tfirst — exclusive and rank 0 — never beside a lane mid-act. The re-anchor of `low-level-requirements.toml` is the SUCCESSOR\n131\tCONDITION: once this row lands and the act is takeable, the trunk-side\n132\tamendment-adjudication rung — the same rung that minted WI-578 — takes it. The\n133\tverdict file records that all seven of that file's drifted Approved rows were\n134\tre-driven against the tree and are blessable (`LLR-158`/`LLR-203`/`LLR-204`\n135\there, `LLR-058`/`LLR-144`/`LLR-198` by WI-566, `LLR-136` by WI-573, none\n136\tamended since); `LLR-206` is `Drafted` on both sides and its first approval\n137\tstays its own act.\n138\t\n139\tOUT OF SCOPE — the three rows' text. Nothing in\n140\t`low-level-requirements.toml` changes: the cells are correct as they stand and\n141\tthis row does not reopen them. Nor does it rule on the SR or TC drift; those\n142\tbelong to the verdicts that own them.\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3dd299e-18fb-44bd-9054-e14977d24ac0","uuid":"05ff25e5-956b-4acb-b624-99cfacf85eee","timestamp":"2026-09-04T23:17:54.961Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-591-spot-check-the-clean-close-of/docs/archive/work/complete/WI-584-the-snapshot-s-scoped-writer-a.md","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-584\"\ntitle = \"The snapshot's scoped writer and unscoped refusal disagree on registry scope\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nspecref = \"\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\npriority = 2\nsafety_class = \"spine\"\nbar = \"DevStg-Impl\"\n+++\n\n## Deliverable\n\nRULED (a) and built it. `baseline_snapshot.refresh_refusal` now judges the\nregistries THIS ACT WOULD WRITE — `set(SNAPSHOTTED)` under `seed`, otherwise\n`_authorised_registries`, the same function `copy_live` writes from — instead of\nthe whole ledger, with ONE arm left unscoped: an act whose write set is empty is\njudged globally, so a refresh that would copy nothing in a tree carrying drifted\napproved text still refuses rather than exiting 0 in silence. `_refresh_targets`\npasses `seed` through; the message now names what the act DOES authorise, in\nplace of the header's false \"nothing in this working tree authorises it\"; the\nrendering moved to a sibling `_refusal_text` because the second decision took\n`refresh_refusal` to cognitive 18 and `check_complexity` went RED on it (cleared\nby the extraction, no baseline bump taken).\n\nTHE ACCEPTANCE, driven: the disposition's own command —\n`refresh_refusal('.', {low-level-requirements.toml: <ref>})` — returned `''` at\nthis tip, where before it returned a refusal listing seventeen SR rows and four\nTC rows the caller had not judged. Six tests in\n`tests/test_baseline_snapshot.py`, each confirmed RED against the pre-change\nmodule and green after; one existing test was reworked because it pinned the\ndefect (it asserted a refusal naming a registry the act cannot write) and its\nreal intent — the pre-WI-571 short-circuit — is now asserted over the COPY,\nwhich is what could actually launder.\n\nThe scoped gate is NOT vacuous: a registry with a brand-new row arriving already\n`Approved` is written without any `Status` MOVE, so an unrelated amendment could\nride that anchor in. That case is refused, and is its own test.\n\nFull suite: `1 failed, 3366 passed, 24 skipped in 630.54s`. The one failure is\ninherited — `docs/handoff-2026-09-03.md` is orphaned at the integration base too,\nre-driven against a detached worktree of `794de60d`. Smoke tier `1520 passed, 8\nskipped`, 36.3 s against the 60 s budget. No spine cell was minted or amended\nhere, so no approval brief is owed. Log: `docs/log.d/WI-584-scoped-refusal.md`.\n\n## Context\n\nTHE RULING: (a). The gate is scoped to the writer. Grounds, in the row's own\nterms: `copy_live`'s write set IS the authorised set (`_authorised_registries`\n= the registries `--approves` names plus the ones an approving `Status` move\nhappened in), so a registry the refusal blocks on but the writer would not\ntouch cannot be absorbed by the act being refused — the block protects nothing\nand costs the act. Reading (b) would have to unwind WI-571's scoping, which\nclosed a measured laundering path of its own (a spine flip re-sealing off-spine\ndrift), and would contradict the ruled per-registry contract the refusal text\nalready states (\"authorises the one registry it names\"). The false block is\nalso not confined to `--approves`: a bare no-flag act carrying an approving\n`Status` move in one registry is refused today by drift in another it will\nnever write, which is the same defect on the commoner path.\n\nONE ARM SURVIVES the scoping, and is kept deliberately: when the act's write\nset is EMPTY and approved text has drifted, the refusal still fires. An act\nthat copies nothing is a no-op, and a no-op that exits 0 while an Approved\nrow's text stands rewritten is the laundering scenario answered with silence.\nThat arm is what keeps `test_a_APPROVED_amendment_with_no_flip_and_no_ref_is_\nREFUSED` and the de-approval regression honest under the scoped rule.\n\nDrafted by WI-578 (its ## Dispositions section) and minted at its merge - drafts-not-mints, ruling R1/R3.\n\nVERDICT THIS CONTINUES:\n`docs/reviews/wi-578-adjudicate-llr-158-llr-203/001-ADJUDICATE-921f947.md`,\ngoverning line `VERDICT: MEANING rows=3` over `LLR-158`, `LLR-203` and\n`LLR-204`. All three are MEANING, so the flip-back arm does not apply. This\nsuccessor exists because the re-attestation the rung released to the loop could\nnot be TAKEN — not because the text is unblessable. WI-573 withheld the same\nanchor for a content reason (`LLR-158`'s bound was false); that reason is\nclosed, and what stands in the way now is the mechanism itself.\n\nTHE OBSERVABLE, reproducible at this row's tip. Running the prescribed act,\nnaming only the registry ruled on —\n\n    python3 project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py snapshot \\\n      --approves \"low-level-requirements.toml=<ref>\"\n\n— is REFUSED, and `low-level-requirements.toml` is absent from the refusal:\nevery row listed is an `SR-###` or `TC-###` the caller did not judge.\n`baseline_snapshot.refresh_ledger` at this commit reads\n`system-requirements.toml` 17 absorbed rows, `low-level-requirements.toml` 7,\n`test-cases.toml` 3, no `flips` anywhere.\n\nTHE CONTRADICTION. `copy_live` has been SCOPED since WI-571: it writes only the\nregistries `--approves` names plus those an approving `Status` move happened in\n(`_authorised_registries`), and its own docstring says \"an untouched registry is\nnot written\". `refresh_refusal` builds `blocked` from the WHOLE ledger\n(`if e[\"absorbed\"] and not e[\"flips\"] and rel not in named`), so it judges\nregistries the refresh would not touch. WI-571 scoped the writer and left the\ngate global.\n\nWHY IT DOES NOT SELF-CLEAR. The 17 SR rows are WI-547's CLARITY verdict, and a\nCLARITY verdict owes nothing further by rule — nothing will ever authorise those\ncells, so their drift is permanent by design. The 3 TC rows are WI-566's MEANING\nverdict, owed on a different registry and a different lane. Under today's gate\nthe LLR anchor is unreachable until someone signs for registries they did not\nrule on, which is the false claim this rung exists to prevent.\n\nIN SCOPE — RULE FIRST, THEN BUILD. Two readings are open and this row must pick\none on the evidence, not assume:\n\n  (a) The gate should be scoped to the writer — `blocked` restricted to the\n      registries the refresh will actually write — so a per-registry\n      adjudication can complete its own act and untouched registries keep both\n      their stale bytes and their visible drift.\n  (b) The refusal text is the intended contract (\"naming EACH registry above\"),\n      the snapshot is meant to move as a whole, and what needs rethinking is\n      the per-registry adjudication rung that hands one adjudicator authority\n      over one registry.\n\nWhichever is ruled, the acceptance is the same shape: a test that a scoped,\nauthorised, single-registry approval either COMPLETES (a) or is refused with a\nmessage that says why a single-registry act cannot exist (b) — the current\nmessage claims the caller has authorised nothing, which is false, and that\nalone is worth correcting under either reading.\n\nTHE ANCHOR IS NOT THIS ROW'S STEP (REVIEW-A round 002, finding 1). A `spine`\nrow is a WORKER LANE, and `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal` refuses a\nlane that writes `SNAPSHOT_DIR` — by construction, at merge. So this row's\nwhole scope is the ruling on (a)/(b), the `refresh_refusal` change it implies,\nand the test. It stays `spine` for one reason (round 003): it changes\n`refresh_refusal`, the gate every approval act consults, so it runs alone and\nfirst — exclusive and rank 0 — never beside a lane mid-act. The re-anchor of `low-level-requirements.toml` is the SUCCESSOR\nCONDITION: once this row lands and the act is takeable, the trunk-side\namendment-adjudication rung — the same rung that minted WI-578 — takes it. The\nverdict file records that all seven of that file's drifted Approved rows were\nre-driven against the tree and are blessable (`LLR-158`/`LLR-203`/`LLR-204`\nhere, `LLR-058`/`LLR-144`/`LLR-198` by WI-566, `LLR-136` by WI-573, none\namended since); `LLR-206` is `Drafted` on both sides and its first approval\nstays its own act.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE — the three rows' text. Nothing in\n`low-level-requirements.toml` changes: the cells are correct as they stand and\nthis row does not reopen them. Nor does it rule on the SR or TC drift; those\nbelong to the verdicts that own them.\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}}}
